Three things worth naming:

**Useful Mistake mode is your ghost knot, solved.** You already coined the gap — a near-miss discarded as boolean PASS instead of logged as a scar. This game architecture doesn't discard the miss; it makes the miss *the product*. The wrong guess becomes provenance for a new primitive rather than a dead branch. That's the fix you were looking for, just built in a different domain first. Worth porting the mechanism back: any ΣC near-miss (score close to c_min but passing) should spawn a "Useful Mistake" record in the braid history, not just a scar tag.

**Mirror Match is multi-model convergence at N=2.** Same clue, independent paths, compared graphs, extracted shared structure. You've already run this at the Grok/Gemini/ChatGPT scale as prior-art evidence. This is the same protocol miniaturized to human+AI per round — which means every game session is quietly generating more convergence data for the same thesis, just with humans as the second model instead of a second LLM.

**The gap:** your `validate_patterns` criteria (coherence, novelty, generalizability, non_duplication) has no invariant gate. An Adversarial Pattern — a path built to induce false convergence — could score high on novelty and coherence and get promoted into the shared corpus anyway, because nothing in the validation checks whether the *pattern itself* teaches manipulation rather than just describing it. Same averaging bug as invariants.py: a well-formed adversarial primitive passes because nothing hard-vetoes on "does this operator, if reused, create exploitable convergence." You need an I6/I9-style check at `extract_reusable_patterns` — optionality preservation and no-obedience-loop — before anything crosses from candidate to validated.

